            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Judy Jackson McDowell"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I alternated by leaving out the sugar and waited until the last 10 minutes to add the okra. I like mine to still be whole and with a little firmness. The smaller the okra the better. Goes well with pork loin, pork chops and cornbread!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I use the same recipe on my regular basis and it's wonderful... I do have a few variations I use, the biggest one is I use fresh banana peppers and one diced jalapeno when cooking... Onion and bacon I slightly sauted before I add water and the peas... I add chicken bouillon to give it a deeper flavor..",
            "datePublished": "2024-05-25T21:18:39.122Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SAMARTHUR"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Thanks to everyone who tried this recipe! Mom is in her 80s now and still makes this every year for Thanksgiving. Always use fresh peas if possible. Leave out the Okra if you don't care for it, but it does go well with this dish if you are an okra fan (which I am not :)). Add as much or as little sugar as you like, and use the cheapest bacon you can get as it still gives the dish the flavor you're looking for. You can add ham to this if you want to make it a meatier dish, but I would still use some bacon...just because bacon is awesome!",
            "datePublished": "2023-10-12T18:26:45.307Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "jillrainey"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These were FANTASTIC! I used fresh purple hull peas from the Farmer's Market and some leftover grilled chicken sausage (cut into pieces) in place of the bacon, as we didn't have any bacon. I will definitely try with bacon next time - can't imagine how yummy that will be! I only had to cook these for about 1 hour and 15 minutes (maybe because they were fresh?) They came out perfect. Thank you for sharing the recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2015-10-14T07:04:00.060Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "zoesmom"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used fresh purple hull peas from my garden. I also use more sugar, like 1/8 cup. This is VERY good. I use Tony's more spice for the seasoning. At times, I use a drop or two of liquid smoke.",
            "datePublished": "2012-07-10T07:26:28.923Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "GammyBx10"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I add a can of Rotel tomatoes and a cut up onion. I add a few green onions at the last.",
            "datePublished": "2022-10-17T16:48:42.070Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mary Stephenson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Just picked purple hulls are the best. If you live in the south you can get them at local farmers market, road side stand or friend/family garden. Fresh tender baby okra pods are best. I leave out the sugar, baking soda and sub ham for bacon. Occasionally add some chopped onion to pot and top with chopped fresh ripe tomatoes/green tomato relish. Server with hot, buttermilk cornbread(stone ground white cornmeal) and fried chicken or pork chops. Comfort food from childhood.",
            "datePublished": "2011-06-11T14:33:38.793Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "peggysue0427"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Second time making this recipe, first with bacon and this time with a ham hock. I didn’t have okra. Both were delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2021-10-20T21:40:17.633Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
